<h1 style=3D"font-size:26px;">Paper Notes</h1>
<p>Hi Anna,</p>
<p>This month, we=E2=80=99re putting three shelf newcomers under the nib. Our=
 product cards compare ink behaviour using the same repeatable blotting test,=
 so you can choose paper with your writing habits in mind.</p>
<section style=3D"padding:16px;margin-bottom:12px;background-color:#ffffff;">
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">Cloudleaf cotton</h2>
<p>Showed the least feathering in our comparison=E2=80=94a promising choice w=
hen crisp letter edges matter most.</p>
</section>
<section style=3D"padding:16px;margin-bottom:12px;background-color:#ffffff;">
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">Rill vellum</h2>
<p>Took longest to dry in our blotting test. Give your ink room to settle bef=
ore turning the page.</p>
</section>
<section style=3D"padding:16px;margin-bottom:12px;background-color:#ffffff;">
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">Slategrain recycled stock</h2>
<p>Produced the strongest show-through with broad nibs. Keep that in mind if =
you like writing on both sides.</p>
</section>
<p>In our closing workshop column, we explain how paper sizing changes absorp=
tion. You=E2=80=99ll also find a worksheet for repeating the test at home wit=
h your own pens and inks.</p>
<p>Happy writing,<br>The Paper Finch team</p>
